"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The surgical pathology report indicates a Gleason 4+4 (ISUP grade group 3) and positive surgical margins, which are concerning findings. Counterbalancing this are the absence of lymph node metastasis and the fact that the seminal vesicles were not invaded. The preoperative PSA was elevated at 8.6. The biopsy-to-prostatectomy difference is noted as missing, which widens uncertainty regarding the true extent of the disease. Nodal status is documented as negative (no lymph node metastasis).\n\nThe primary driver for prognosis is the positive surgical margins and the high preoperative PSA. The lack of lymph node metastasis is a favorable finding. Information that would significantly revise this prognosis includes the postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory. A higher postoperative PSA would suggest a poorer outcome. Since the postoperative PSA and follow-up are not documented, this remains a significant unknown that impacts the certainty of the prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 46.71 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 37.37–56.05 months; its coverage has not been validated."
